            <ArticleTitle>Differentiation Between Sickle Cell Anemia and S/β⁰ Thalassemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e79-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1309/LMKW5VWNUK26LKAX</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL HISTORY PATIENT" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">37-year-old man of half African and half Italian ethnicity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CHIEF COMPLAINT" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Sickle cell crisis (SCC).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The patient had severe pain in his lower back that radiated to both thighs. He had been admitted twice in the previous 2 weeks at another hospital due to SCC. Each time, he had been discharged with a prescription for oxycodone. His condition did not respond to the pain medicine; his pain remained uncontrollable.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEDICAL HISTORY" NlmCategory="METHODS">The patient has had multiple sickle cell crises since childhood and is a former smoker. He denied alcohol consumption or illegal drug use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PHYSICAL EXAMINATION FINDINGS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The patient had normal vital signs. No splenomegaly was present.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FAMILY HISTORY" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Both parents carry sickle cell thalassemia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRINCIPAL LABORATORY FINDINGS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Table 1, Table 2, and Image 1.</AbstractText>
